//// emilia/tokens — the typed `Token` enum: the entire authored surface.
////
//// A consumer writes styles by LISTING typed tokens; never by writing CSS
//// strings. Each variant maps to a fixed CSS declaration via `tokenToCss`
//// (in `emilia.bp`). Unknown tokens are type errors at the call site, not
//// runtime fall-throughs.
////
//// V1 SHAPE (post enum-sections, landed in feat `bd5c69d`). The `Token`
//// enum uses NESTED SECTIONS with path access (`.Text.Bold`,
//// `.Color.Red.500`, `.Pad.X.4`). Sections compose to any depth; numeric
//// leaves are valid inside a section (the language extension's two
//// relaxations over today's enum grammar). Modifier variants
//// (`Hover([Token])`, `Md([Token])`, …) carry a recursive `Token[]`
//// payload — siblings of section heads at the top level. Front 34 widened
//// them from six to 83; the full map is under MODIFIERS below.
////
//// SECTIONS:
////   Text     — Bold/Italic/Underline/LineThrough, Left/Center/Right/Justify,
////              + Size { Xs, Sm, Base, Lg, Xl, X2xl, X3xl, X4xl }
////   Font     — Sans/Serif/Mono + Weight { Light, Normal, Medium, Bold, Black }
////   Color    — front 33: the 26-family x 11-shade grid — the 17 chromatic
////              families Red..Rose and the 9 neutral Slate..Taupe, each
////              50,100,…,900,950 — plus White/Black/Transparent/Current/
////              Inherit and the Hex(string) escape
////   Bg       — front 33: the Color sub-section is the same 26 x 11 grid on
////              `background-color`, plus White/Black/Transparent/Current/
////              Inherit. The legacy Red/Blue/Gray leaves beside it emit the
////              `background` SHORTHAND and keep their pre-33 output — front 39
////              did NOT fold them in, because changing what a compiling path
////              means is not a thing one front does to another's surface.
////              front 39: seven keyword sub-sections beside them, one per
////              `§ 10` property group that is not a colour — Attachment,
////              Clip, Origin, Pos, Repeat, Size and Image. `Pos` is short so
////              that `.Bg.Pos.*` stays four segments and reads apart from
////              `.Layout.Position.*`; `Repeat.None` is `no-repeat` and does
////              not repeat its section's word; `Clip.Text` is the one clip
////              value that is not a `*-box`. Gradients are NOT here — see
////              the Gradient section below
////   Pad      — front 35: nine directions — All, X, Y, T, R, B, L and the
////              logical pair S/E — over the 35-leaf scale: the thirty
////              multipliers 0..96, the Px step, and Half { 0, 1, 2, 3 } for
////              the 0.5/1.5/2.5/3.5 rungs (a numeric leaf is a run of digits,
////              so `0.5` cannot be one). A leaf is a MULTIPLIER of `--spacing`
////              and never a resolved length: `.Pad.All.4` is
////              `padding:calc(var(--spacing) * 4)`
////   Margin   — front 35: the same nine directions and the same scale, plus
////              Auto on EVERY direction — a VALUE on the ladder, so
////              `.Margin.X.Auto` is `margin-left:auto;margin-right:auto` —
////              and a Neg sub-section per direction, upstream's `-mt-4`.
////              Neg carries no 0 and no Auto, and its Half is { 1, 2, 3 }:
////              `spacingHalf(-0)` is `spacingHalf(0)`, so the -0.5 rung is the
////              one front 54's ladder cannot spell and emilia will not spell a
////              second `calc(var(--spacing) * …)` to reach it
////   Size     — front 35: thirteen sub-sections over §8.1-§8.7 — W, H, Both
////              (upstream's size-*, two properties from one leaf), MinW, MaxW,
////              MinH, MaxH and the six logical Inline/Block forms. Four kinds
////              of leaf: a NUMBER is the spacing ladder, a Frac is a
////              percentage carried by name (`1/2` is no identifier), a KEYWORD
////              is an intrinsic size or a viewport unit (which differs by
////              axis — w-screen is 100vw, h-screen is 100vh), and a NAMED
////              width is the theme's container ladder
////              (`.Size.MaxW.Md` → `max-width:var(--container-md)`), with
////              MaxW.Screen.* reading the breakpoint ladder the same way
////   Space    — front 35: `space-x-*` / `space-y-*`, over the same scale with
////              the same Neg — but the declaration is on the element's
////              CHILDREN, so this section's dispatcher answers a `Sheet` whose
////              selector is `& > :not(:last-child)` rather than a declaration
////              string. XReverse/YReverse set the upstream `--tw-space-*-
////              reverse` custom property. Front 40's `divide-*` separates the
////              same children and must call `siblingSelector()`
////   Layout   — front 36: the `§ 5` vocabulary. The eleven display values are
////              the section's OWN leaves (Block, InlineBlock, Inline,
////              InlineFlex, InlineGrid, Contents, FlowRoot, ListItem, Hidden,
////              Flex, Grid), so `.Layout.Flex` keeps its pre-36 spelling, and
////              one sub-section per property group sits beside them:
////              Position, and Inset over the nine directions front 35's Pad
////              and Margin use — All, X, Y, T, R, B, L, S, E — on the same
////              scale, answering the same `spacing(n)`/`spacingHalf(n)`, plus
////              Auto, Full and Frac { Half, Third, TwoThirds }. `inset` is a
////              real CSS shorthand so All is ONE declaration; X and Y expand
////              to two, which is how §5.17 prints `inset-x-0`; then Overflow
////              and Overscroll (a shorthand plus an X and a Y longhand each),
////              Visibility (`invisible` is the utility name, `hidden` the CSS
////              value), Z — the one numeric family here that is NOT a length,
////              a bare integer — and Isolation; then Float and Clear (whose
////              logical values are `inline-start`/`inline-end`, not
////              `start`/`end`), Object { Fit, Pos } and Aspect; and Columns,
////              BreakAfter, BreakBefore, BreakInside, Box and BoxDecoration.
////              The three Break sub-sections are FLAT and not `Break { After,
////              Before, Inside }`: a section head whose name equals a
////              top-level payload variant's breaks that variant's payload
////              projection, and front 34 carries `After(inner: Token[])` and
////              `Before(inner: Token[])`. See AGENTS.md § Maintainer rules.
////              Columns' named widths are the THEME's container ladder —
////              `.Layout.Columns.Md` is `columns:var(--container-md)`, the
////              same reference `.Size.MaxW.Md` reads — and no leaf in the
////              section spells a `rem`
////   Flex     — front 37: the flex CONTAINER and the flex ITEM. Seven
////              direction/wrap leaves (Row/RowReverse/Col/ColReverse/Wrap/
////              WrapReverse/NoWrap), the `flex` shorthand as Value{One,Auto,
////              Initial,None} — `Value` and not `Flex`, a section carrying no
////              sub-section of its own name — Grow{0,1}, Shrink{0,1},
////              Basis over front 35's scale plus Auto/Full/Frac, and
////              Order{1..12,First,Last,None}. Beside them the WHOLE
////              alignment family of §6.16-§6.24: Items, Justify,
////              JustifyItems, JustifySelf, AlignSelf, Content, PlaceContent,
////              PlaceItems, PlaceSelf. It stays here although it applies to
////              GRID too — `align-items` and `justify-content` were spelled
////              `.Flex.*` before front 37 and renaming a token that compiles
////              is what the milestone forbids; a grid container writes
////              `.Flex.Justify.Center` and gets the right CSS. `AlignSelf`
////              and the FLAT Place* trio rather than `Self` and `Place{…}`:
////              `Self` is a language KEYWORD and neither parses. The legacy
////              Gap{1,2,4,8} stays and now emits what `.Gap.All.N` emits.
////              Only Basis is a length, and it answers `spacing(n)`
////   Grid     — front 37: §6.8-§6.14. Cols/Rows {1..12, None, Subgrid},
////              Col/Row { Auto, Span{1..12,Full}, Start{1..13,Auto},
////              End{1..13,Auto} } — Start and End run to 13 because a
////              twelve-column grid has THIRTEEN LINES — Flow{Row,Col,Dense,
////              RowDense,ColDense}, AutoCols/AutoRows{Auto,Min,Max,Fr}.
////              Nothing here is a length: a count, a span and a line number
////              are integers. `gridRepeat(n)` builds
////              `repeat(N, minmax(0, 1fr))` and `gridFr()` the
////              `minmax(0, 1fr)` inside it, each spelled once
////   Gap      — front 37: §6.15, TOP LEVEL rather than a sub-section of
////              Flex, because `gap`, `column-gap` and `row-gap` separate the
////              items of a grid exactly as they separate the items of a flex
////              row. All/X/Y over front 35's full scale, answering front
////              54's `spacing(n)`/`spacingHalf(n)`, so `.Gap.All.4` and
////              `.Pad.All.4` are the same length by construction
////   Border   — front 40: the whole of `§ 11.1`-`§ 11.4`. W carries five
////              widths (0,1,2,4,8) on the shorthand AND on eight directional
////              sub-sections — the axes X/Y, the sides T/R/B/L and the
////              logical pair S/E, which is `border-inline-start-width`. Style
////              is the six `border-style` values. Color is front 33's full
////              26 x 11 grid plus White/Black/Transparent/Current/Inherit and
////              the Hex(string) escape, every cell through paletteVar — the
////              pre-40 stub carried two families at three shades and its
////              dispatcher DISCARDED THE SHADE. Rounded is the ten-leaf
////              radius ladder {None,Xs,Sm,Md,Lg,Xl,X2xl,X3xl,X4xl,Full} on
////              the shorthand and on fourteen directional sub-sections — the
////              four sides, the four physical corners Tl/Tr/Br/Bl and the six
////              logical ones S/E/Ss/Se/Es/Ee. A side form emits TWO corner
////              properties and a corner form ONE. Every radius but None and
////              Full REFERENCES front 54's `--radius-*` ladder; the two that
////              stay literal (`0`, `9999px`) are the two upstream prints
////              literally. 492 leaves
////   Outline  — front 40: `§ 11.5`-`§ 11.8` — W{0,1,2,4,8},
////              Style{Solid,Dashed,Dotted,Double,None}, Color over the full
////              grid, and Offset{0,1,2,4,8} with Neg{1,2,4,8} for upstream's
////              `-outline-offset-N`. An outline is painted outside the border
////              box and TAKES NO SPACE, which is why it and not a border is
////              how a focus ring is drawn. `Outline.Style.None` is the trap:
////              it emits `outline:2px solid transparent;outline-offset:2px`,
////              a TRANSPARENT outline rather than an absent one, and never
////              `outline-style:none`. 310 leaves
////   Ring     — front 40: the box-shadow half of the focus story.
////              W{0,1,2,4,8} sets `--tw-ring-shadow` AND the composed
////              `box-shadow` that lists it beside `var(--tw-shadow)`, so a
////              ring COMPOSES with Effect.Shadow instead of overwriting it.
////              Color and Offset.Color are the full grid through paletteVar;
////              Offset.W is `--tw-ring-offset-width`; Inset is the
////              `--tw-ring-inset` flag. Its dispatcher is `ringTokenToSheet`,
////              front 56's second shape. 593 leaves
////   Divide   — front 40: `divide-*`, which declares on the element's
////              CHILDREN rather than on the element — a border on every child
////              but the last. X/Y carry the five widths as the zero-then-width
////              pair upstream emits; Color and Style join the SAME child rule;
////              XReverse/YReverse set the reverse custom properties. Its
////              dispatcher is `divideTokenToSheet`, and its selector is front
////              35's `siblingSelector()` — CALLED, never re-spelled, because
////              `space-*` separates the same children the same way. 309 leaves
////   Effect   — Shadow{Sm,Md,Lg,Xl}, Opacity{0,25,50,75,100}
////   Gradient — front 39: a TOP-LEVEL section, not a sub-section of Bg — a
////              stop sets a custom property and not `background-image`, and a
////              stop path is four segments already. To { T, Tr, R, Br, B, Bl,
////              L, Tl } is the DIRECTION, spelled `to top right` and never
////              `to top-right`; `Stop` is the terminal colour. Tailwind
////              spells both with the word `to` while they set unrelated
////              things; emilia does not. From / Via / Stop each carry the
////              whole of front 33's grid — 26 families x 11 shades plus
////              White/Black/Transparent/Current/Inherit, 291 leaves each —
////              and the colour half of every one is paletteVar(family,
////              shade), so a stop and a background reference ONE custom
////              property. Token ORDER is load-bearing: Via writes a
////              three-stop list and From a two-stop one, last one wins, and
////              Stop writes no list at all so that it cannot overwrite Via's
////
//// WRAPPERS:
////   .Alpha(percent, [Token]) → front 33: rewrites each inner declaration's
////                              VALUE into `color-mix(in oklab, <value> N%,
////                              transparent)`, which is upstream's `/N`
////                              opacity suffix (`bg-red-500/50`). Opacity
////                              cannot be a leaf under the family — the shade
////                              is already the leaf and a second numeric level
////                              would multiply 26 x 11 x 21 leaves — and a
////                              payload leaf nested in a section cannot be
////                              constructed, so it is a TOP-LEVEL variant with
////                              a builtin-typed field.
////
//// MODIFIERS — front 34's table. A modifier is the only way a token reaches
//// a state, a breakpoint or a pseudo-element. Each is a top-level variant
//// carrying `inner: Token[]` and answers one `Variant` (an at-rule and a
//// selector template with exactly one `&`); `nestVariant` in `output.bp`
//// applies it, and nothing here builds a brace. `Nth`/`NthLast` carry an
//// `i32` beside the list; `Important` is the one that is a FLAG, not a
//// variant. `.group`/`.peer` are classes the CONSUMER's markup carries.
////
////   breakpoints — min-width, read from the theme's `--breakpoint-*` ladder
////     .Sm([Token])               sm:              → @media (width >= <--breakpoint-sm>){…}
////     .Md([Token])               md:              → @media (width >= <--breakpoint-md>){…}
////     .Lg([Token])               lg:              → @media (width >= <--breakpoint-lg>){…}
////     .Xl([Token])               xl:              → @media (width >= <--breakpoint-xl>){…}
////     .X2xl([Token])             2xl:             → @media (width >= <--breakpoint-2xl>){…}
////
////   breakpoints — max-width, the same ladder read the other way
////     .MaxSm([Token])            max-sm:          → @media (width < <--breakpoint-sm>){…}
////     .MaxMd([Token])            max-md:          → @media (width < <--breakpoint-md>){…}
////     .MaxLg([Token])            max-lg:          → @media (width < <--breakpoint-lg>){…}
////     .MaxXl([Token])            max-xl:          → @media (width < <--breakpoint-xl>){…}
////     .MaxX2xl([Token])          max-2xl:         → @media (width < <--breakpoint-2xl>){…}
////
////   dark mode and the other media features
////     .Dark([Token])             dark:            → darkAtRule(th){darkSelector(th){…}} — all three strategies
////     .Print([Token])            print:           → @media print{…}
////     .Portrait([Token])         portrait:        → @media (orientation: portrait){…}
////     .Landscape([Token])        landscape:       → @media (orientation: landscape){…}
////     .MotionSafe([Token])       motion-safe:     → @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference){…}
////     .MotionReduce([Token])     motion-reduce:   → @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce){…}
////     .ContrastMore([Token])     contrast-more:   → @media (prefers-contrast: more){…}
////     .ContrastLess([Token])     contrast-less:   → @media (prefers-contrast: less){…}
////     .ForcedColors([Token])     forced-colors:   → @media (forced-colors: active){…}
////
////   interaction state
////     .Hover([Token])            hover:           → @media (hover: hover){&:hover{…}}
////     .Focus([Token])            focus:           → &:focus{…}
////     .FocusWithin([Token])      focus-within:    → &:focus-within{…}
////     .FocusVisible([Token])     focus-visible:   → &:focus-visible{…}
////     .Active([Token])           active:          → &:active{…}
////     .Visited([Token])          visited:         → &:visited{…}
////     .Target([Token])           target:          → &:target{…}
////     .Open([Token])             open:            → &:is(:open, :popover-open){…}
////     .Inert([Token])            inert:           → &:is([inert], [inert] *){…}
////
////   form state
////     .Disabled([Token])         disabled:        → &:disabled{…}
////     .Enabled([Token])          enabled:         → &:enabled{…}
////     .Checked([Token])          checked:         → &:checked{…}
////     .Indeterminate([Token])    indeterminate:   → &:indeterminate{…}
////     .Default([Token])          default:         → &:default{…}
////     .Optional([Token])         optional:        → &:optional{…}
////     .Required([Token])         required:        → &:required{…}
////     .Valid([Token])            valid:           → &:valid{…}
////     .Invalid([Token])          invalid:         → &:invalid{…}
////     .UserValid([Token])        user-valid:      → &:user-valid{…}
////     .UserInvalid([Token])      user-invalid:    → &:user-invalid{…}
////     .InRange([Token])          in-range:        → &:in-range{…}
////     .OutOfRange([Token])       out-of-range:    → &:out-of-range{…}
////     .PlaceholderShown([Token]) placeholder-shown: → &:placeholder-shown{…}
////     .Autofill([Token])         autofill:        → &:autofill{…}
////     .ReadOnly([Token])         read-only:       → &:read-only{…}
////
////   structural position
////     .First([Token])            first:           → &:first-child{…}
////     .Last([Token])             last:            → &:last-child{…}
////     .Only([Token])             only:            → &:only-child{…}
////     .Odd([Token])              odd:             → &:nth-child(odd){…}
////     .Even([Token])             even:            → &:nth-child(even){…}
////     .FirstOfType([Token])      first-of-type:   → &:first-of-type{…}
////     .LastOfType([Token])       last-of-type:    → &:last-of-type{…}
////     .OnlyOfType([Token])       only-of-type:    → &:only-of-type{…}
////     .Empty([Token])            empty:           → &:empty{…}
////     .Nth(index, [Token])       nth-N:           → &:nth-child(N){…}
////     .NthLast(index, [Token])   nth-last-N:      → &:nth-last-child(N){…}
////
////   pseudo-elements
////     .Before([Token])           before:          → &::before{…}
////     .After([Token])            after:           → &::after{…}
////     .FirstLetter([Token])      first-letter:    → &::first-letter{…}
////     .FirstLine([Token])        first-line:      → &::first-line{…}
////     .Placeholder([Token])      placeholder:     → &::placeholder{…}
////     .File([Token])             file:            → &::file-selector-button{…}
////     .Marker([Token])           marker:          → & ::marker{…}
////     .Selection([Token])        selection:       → & ::selection{…}
////     .Backdrop([Token])         backdrop:        → &::backdrop{…}
////
////   parent state — the `.group` class is the consumer's, never emilia's
////     .GroupHover([Token])       group-hover:     → &:is(:where(.group):hover *){…}
////     .GroupFocus([Token])       group-focus:     → &:is(:where(.group):focus *){…}
////     .GroupActive([Token])      group-active:    → &:is(:where(.group):active *){…}
////     .GroupVisited([Token])     group-visited:   → &:is(:where(.group):visited *){…}
////     .GroupDisabled([Token])    group-disabled:  → &:is(:where(.group):disabled *){…}
////     .GroupOpen([Token])        group-open:      → &:is(:where(.group):open *){…}
////
////   sibling state — the `.peer` class is the consumer's, never emilia's
////     .PeerHover([Token])        peer-hover:      → &:is(:where(.peer):hover ~ *){…}
////     .PeerFocus([Token])        peer-focus:      → &:is(:where(.peer):focus ~ *){…}
////     .PeerActive([Token])       peer-active:     → &:is(:where(.peer):active ~ *){…}
////     .PeerChecked([Token])      peer-checked:    → &:is(:where(.peer):checked ~ *){…}
////     .PeerInvalid([Token])      peer-invalid:    → &:is(:where(.peer):invalid ~ *){…}
////     .PeerRequired([Token])     peer-required:   → &:is(:where(.peer):required ~ *){…}
////     .PeerDisabled([Token])     peer-disabled:   → &:is(:where(.peer):disabled ~ *){…}
////     .PeerPlaceholderShown([Token]) peer-placeholder-shown: → &:is(:where(.peer):placeholder-shown ~ *){…}
////
////   writing direction and descent
////     .Rtl([Token])              rtl:             → [dir="rtl"] &{…}
////     .Ltr([Token])              ltr:             → [dir="ltr"] &{…}
////     .Children([Token])         *:               → :is(& > *){…}
////     .Descendants([Token])      **:              → :is(& *){…}
////
////   the priority wrapper — not a variant, a flag on every rule it wraps
////     .Important([Token])        …!               → !important on every declaration it wraps

pub type Token {
    Text {
        Bold,
        Italic,
        Underline,
        LineThrough,
        Left,
        Center,
        Right,
        Justify,
        Size {
            Xs,
            Sm,
            Base,
            Lg,
            Xl,
            X2xl,
            X3xl,
            X4xl,
        }
    }

    Font {
        Sans,
        Serif,
        Mono,
        Weight {
            Light,
            Normal,
            Medium,
            Bold,
            Black,
        }
    }

    Color {
        Red     { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Orange  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Amber   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Yellow  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Lime    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Green   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Emerald { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Teal    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Cyan    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Sky     { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Blue    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Indigo  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Violet  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Purple  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Fuchsia { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Pink    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Rose    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Slate   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Gray    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Zinc    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Neutral { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Stone   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Mauve   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Olive   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Mist    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        Taupe   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
        White,
        Black,
        Transparent,
        Current,
        Inherit,
        Hex(value: string),
    }

    Bg {
        Color {
            Red     { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Orange  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Amber   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Yellow  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Lime    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Green   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Emerald { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Teal    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Cyan    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Sky     { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Blue    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Indigo  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Violet  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Purple  { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Fuchsia { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Pink    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Rose    { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
            Slate   { 50, 100, 200, 300, 400, 500, 600, 700, 800, 900, 950 }
